{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "2 - Twirp API",
  "steps": [
    {
      "directory": "proto/services",
      "description": "## Twirp API service definition protobufs\n### Twirp\n\n[Twirp](https://twitchtv.github.io/twirp/docs/intro.html) is the standard for internal api requests at github. It is an rpc framework that uses protobufs to define services, endpoints, and request and response arguments.\n\nThe public interface of the twirp api is defined by a set of protocol buffers (protobufs). These define both the available API methods, and their input and return types. From these definitions we can auto-generate ruby and go clients for external use, and define the server interfaces for the endpoints in the twirp server.\n\nThese protobufs live in the `proto/services` directory. We can define multiple \"services\" on the same twirp server, so we can create logical groupings of endpoints into services.\n\n\n### Adding Twirp endpoints\n\nThe basic process of adding a twirp endpoint looks like:\n\n- define the interface for the endpoint as protobufs\n- run code generation scripts to generate the golang twirp handler interface, and the ruby client code to interface with it\n- implement the business logic for the endpoint handler in go\n- deploy the endpoint\n- publish a new version of the rubygem, and include it in the monolith\n- use the rubygem to make requests from the monolith to your new endpoint"
    },
    {
      "file": "proto/services/devicetokens/service.proto",
      "description": "## Service Definition\n\nThis protobuf defines the service as a whole. This \"service\" is for handling device tokens. If you need a new service you would add it in a new service in `proto/services`. But if you just need a new endpoint relating to device tokens you would add it here.",
      "pattern": "service DeviceTokens {"
    },
    {
      "file": "proto/services/devicetokens/service.proto",
      "description": "## RPC Endpoints\n\nAnd here you can see the definition of a single endpoint within the service, including its arguments and return types.\n\n`ReplaceDeviceTokensRequest` is an internal message type as shown below, and it returns an \"empty\" `google.protobuf.Empty` response which is imported at the top of the file.\n\nIf you want to add an additional endpoint that works with device tokens it probably goes here.",
      "pattern": "rpc ReplaceDeviceTokens"
    },
    {
      "file": "proto/services/devicetokens/service.proto",
      "description": "### protobufs\n\nThe \"messages\" (i.e. arguments and return values) are defined using [`proto3`](https://developers.google.com/protocol-buffers/docs/proto3). The syntax is the same as that used for [hydro messages](https://hydro.githubapp.com/docs/adding_an_event) but these will be used in the requests and responses for your twirp endpoint, not in hydro.",
      "pattern": "message DeviceToken {"
    },
    {
      "file": "script/protoc",
      "description": "## `script/protoc`\n\nThis script is what you would run after making a change to the services.\n\nIt will:\n- generate the `proto/services/$name/service.pb.go` and `proto/services/$name/service.twirp.go` files that you will use to implement the go backend\n- generate the `ruby/lib/notifyd/proto/services/` files that will allow the rubygem to talk to the backend\n\nTry it:\n\n>> script/protoc",
      "line": 2
    },
    {
      "file": "proto/services/devicetokens/service.twirp.go",
      "description": "## Generated go code\n\nThis file (`service.twirp.go`) and `service.pb.go` are what's generated by `script/protoc`. You shouldn't edit these but you will import them in your go api handler when you import the `services/devicetokens` package.",
      "line": 2
    },
    {
      "file": "proto/services/devicetokens/service.twirp.go",
      "description": "This interface is the main thing you are interested in. It defines the service and each of its rpc methods which should match the definition you wrote in the protobufs.\n\nYour job is to implement a handler that matches this interface.",
      "pattern": "type .* interface {"
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/api/devicetokensserver/server.go",
      "description": "## Handler implementation\n\nHere you can see where we implement the `ReplaceDeviceTokens` rpc endpoint that we said our twirp api supported via the interface. And you can see its argument (`pb.ReplaceDeviceTokenRequest`) type comes from the generated go code.",
      "pattern": "func \\(s \\*Server\\) ReplaceDeviceTokens\\("
    },
    {
      "file": "cmd/api/main.go",
      "description": "## Hooking up the handler\n\nHere you can see where we finally hookup the device token implementation in our api.",
      "pattern": "devicetokens\\.NewDeviceTokensServer\\("
    },
    {
      "file": "ruby/lib/notifyd/proto/services/devicetokens/service_twirp.rb",
      "description": "## Ruby client\n\nAnd here's the generated ruby client code. The `DeviceTokensClient` wraps the entire service which you can see defined above, and has a matching class for the `DevicedTokensService` and the rpc endpoint `ReplaceDeviceTokens`.",
      "pattern": "client_for DeviceTokensService"
    },
    {
      "file": "ruby/lib/notifyd/client.rb",
      "description": "## Notifyd::Client\n\n`Notifyd::Client` is the main class we expose for the monolith/integrators to use.\n\nBecause each of the twirp \"services\" has a separate client class, we initialize and expose them on the main client like this, so that an integrator can just do something like:\n\n```ruby\nnotifyd = Notifyd::Client.new(notify_url: \"http://notifyd...\")\nnotifyd.device_tokens.replace_device_tokens(...)\n```\n\nFor convenience we expose an initialized client for each of the twirp \"services\" like this.",
      "pattern": "DeviceTokensClient\\.new\\("
    },
    {
      "file": "ruby/test/integration/client_integration_test.rb",
      "description": "## Ruby tests and example code\n\nAnd via the test code you can see how we can make a request to our endpoint using the autogenerated ruby code.\n\n",
      "pattern": "def test_client_can_request_from_server"
    }
  ]
}